==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|erda|lang=goh}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*erþō}}. Compare Dutch {{term|aarde|lang=nl}}, West Frisian {{term|ierde|lang=fy}}, English {{term|earth|lang=en}}, Danish {{term|jord|lang=da}}, Gothic {{term|𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐌰|tr=airþa|lang=got}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʔeːɐ̯də/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|de-Erde.ogg|audio|lang=de}}
* {{rhymes|eːɐ̯də|lang=de}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|f|Erde|Erden}}

# the planet [[Earth]]
# [[soil]]
#: {{usex|Sie nahm eine Handvoll '''Erde'''.|t=She took a handful of soil.|lang=de|inline=1}}
# the [[ground]]
#: {{usex|Er fiel auf die '''Erde'''.|t=He fell on the ground.|lang=de|inline=1}}

====Declension====
{{de-decl-noun-f|n|n}}

====Usage notes====
The old dative form {{term|Erden|lang=de}} is only used in the phrase {{term|auf Erden|lang=de}} ("on earth"), which has a religious or solemn ring to it. In most contexts it is preferable to say ''auf der Erde''.

